C++ Multiple Inheritance – Academic, Sports and Student

Description

This C++ program demonstrates multiple inheritance.

In multiple inheritance, a single derived class inherits from more than one base class.

Here:

- "Academic" is one base class.
- "Sports" is another base class.
- "Student" inherits from both classes.

Class Hierarchy

Academic       Sports
    \           /
     \         /
       Student

1. Academic Class

The "Academic" class stores academic marks.

- "academicMarks" stores academic marks.
- The constructor initializes the marks.
- "showAcademic()" displays the academic marks.

2. Sports Class

The "Sports" class stores sports marks.

- "sportsMarks" stores sports marks.
- The constructor initializes the marks.
- "showSports()" displays the sports marks.

3. Student Class

The "Student" class inherits from both "Academic" and "Sports".

class Student : public Academic, public Sports

Its constructor calls both base class constructors.

The "showTotal()" function calculates and displays the total marks:

Total = Academic Marks + Sports Marks

Main Function

A "Student" object is created with:

Student student(80, 15);

Where:

- Academic marks = "80"
- Sports marks = "15"

The program displays both marks and their total.

Output

Academic Marks: 80
Sports Marks: 15
Total Marks: 95

OOP Concept Used

Multiple Inheritance

       Academic       Sports
           \           /
            \         /
              Student

The "Student" class inherits properties and functions from both "Academic" and "Sports".
